Revision 13 — 2026-05-17
=========================================================

Summary
---------------------------------------------------------
Implemented multiline comment (/* */) syntax highlighting with
state-machine tracking across rows. Fixed editor_safe_realloc
for size==0 (latent MinGW crash), added HL_ML_COMMENT colour
in render.c.
---------------------------------------------------------
Detail
---------------------------------------------------------

[1] src/syntax.c — editor_update_syntax: multiline comment state machine
    Rewrote the function with three priority layers:
      1. Multiline comment state machine (highest priority)
         - Checks in_comment flag inherited from previous row
         - While in_comment: paints HL_ML_COMMENT, checks for
           closing delimiter mce, transitions out on match
         - When not in_comment: checks for opening delimiter mcs,
           transitions in on match
         - Both paths use `continue` to skip keyword/single-line
           matching while inside a comment
      2. Single-line comment detection (unchanged logic)
      3. Keyword matching at word boundaries (unchanged logic)
    Added recursive propagation: when highlight_open_comment
    changes, editor_update_syntax is called on the next row to
    immediately re-highlight it (avoids stale colours until the
    next keystroke).
    Moved render_size == 0 guard BEFORE safe_realloc to avoid
    calling realloc(non-NULL, 0) which crashes on MinGW.

[2] src/render.c — editor_syntax_to_color: add HL_ML_COMMENT case
    Before : (no case — fell through to default white)
    After  : case HL_ML_COMMENT: return 36;  // cyan
    Why    : Without this, multiline comment bytes were rendered
             as default white instead of comment-cyan.

[3] src/common.c — editor_safe_realloc: handle size==0
    Before : realloc(ptr, 0) → frees and returns NULL on MSVCRT,
             which was treated as OOM → exit(1).
    After  : size==0 frees ptr and returns NULL directly.
    Why    : Latent crash. Other callers never pass 0 today, but
             the function should handle it gracefully instead of
             calling exit(1) on a valid realloc size.

